[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should browser logs actually go in production? → Buffer + manual export (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Buffered with manual export — good, that keeps the data on the user's machine until they choose to hand it over.

  That choice makes redaction the load-bearing safety decision, since an exported buffer is a file someone might paste
  into a ticket or email. Concretely: today [38;5;153mapp.js[39m logs [38;5;153musername[39m on every attempt, and [38;5;153mpassword[39m is sitting right next to
  it in the same scope.

  A key-based denylist (redact anything named [38;5;153mpassword[39m, [38;5;153mtoken[39m, [38;5;153msecret[39m, [38;5;153mauth[39m, [38;5;153mcookie[39m, plus a scan for obvious
  secret-shaped values) is the pragmatic standard and catches every realistic case in a codebase this size, but it's
  leaky by construction — a field named [38;5;153mpw[39m or a password nested inside an object under a benign key gets through. An
  allowlist inverts that: nothing is logged unless the field is explicitly registered as safe, so it fails closed, but
  every new log call site needs a field declared, and in practice that friction pushes people toward logging
  interpolated strings instead, which defeats structured logging entirely. Strings-only sidesteps the whole mechanism
  but moves the entire burden to each call site, which is the status quo that got [38;5;153musername[39m logged in the first place.

  This one is cheap to tighten later and expensive to loosen, so erring strict costs little.

  My recommendation is the denylist, plus a hard structural rule that the logger never accepts a password value at all —
  the login call site passes a redacted payload, not the raw form data.
